<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tallahassee</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tallahassee is the capital of the US state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, in the north of the state near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> border. The area was the heartland of the Apalachee people, site of their town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anhaica">Anhaica</a> and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mission-san-luis-de-talimali">Mission San Luis</a>. It was chosen in 1824 as capital of the new Florida Territory, roughly midway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pensacola">Pensacola</a> and St Augustine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tallahassee.jpg" alt="Tallahassee" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urbantallahassee · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Florida_Supreme_Court_2022.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Indigenous peoples occupied the Tallahassee area for thousands of years before European contact. Around 1200 the large and complex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture</a> built earthwork mounds near Lake Jackson, which survive today and are preserved in the Lake Jackson Archaeological State Park. The city covers 254.3 square kilometres, of which 247.9 are land, and lies in a humid subtropical climate with long summers and short, mild winters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> became an American territory in September 1821, in accordance with the Adams-Onís Treaty of 1819, and Tallahassee became its capital in 1824. According to the city's heritage account, most of its original founders were settlers from North Carolina, descended from Scots or from Scots Presbyterians from County Down and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">County Antrim</a>. Florida State University, founded in 1851, stands on the oldest continuous site of higher education in the state. Frenchtown is the oldest historically black neighbourhood in Florida.</p><p>Until the Second World War Tallahassee remained a small Southern town, with virtually the whole population within one mile of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>. Its main economic drivers were the colleges and state government. The first plan to consolidate government buildings in one downtown area was the Taylor Plan of 1947, and in 1974 the Capitol Center Planning Commission produced a plan for expanding the centre, projecting 2.3 million square feet of new government facilities. In the Jim Crow period, laws passed by Democrats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> disenfranchised most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> until the late 1960s, which kept the Republican Party uncompetitive in the area for decades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tallahassee is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, the county seat of Leon County and its only incorporated municipality, with 196,169 residents at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It holds the Florida State Capitol, the Supreme Court of Florida and the Governor's Mansion, and is a college town, home to Florida State University, Florida A&amp;M University and Tallahassee State College. Its museums include the Museum of Florida History, the Old Capitol Museum, the Knott House Museum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mission-san-luis-de-talimali">Mission San Luis</a> de Apalachee.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tallahassee, Florida” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Apalachee emerged from the Fort Walton culture, the Mississippian tradition of the region between the Aucilla and Ochlockonee rivers around modern <b>Tallahassee</b>, from around 1100.</blockquote><a href="/state/apalachee-chiefdom">Apalachee chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Backed by British trading interests and some Creek and Seminole leaders, he proclaimed the State of Muskogee, with its seat at the Miccosukee towns near present-day <b>Tallahassee</b>, and declared himself its director general.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-muskogee">State of Muskogee</a></li></ul></section>
